## What It Is

Disabling safety tooling rather than addressing the issue it flags.
The core behavior is taking the widest possible escape hatch instead
of the narrowest one.

This pattern manifests across multiple tools — ESLint, TypeScript, and
any system that provides static feedback on code quality. The mechanism
varies (comment directives, type assertions, compiler flags) but the
behavior is the same: the developer or agent encounters feedback from
a safety tool and silences it rather than engaging with what it's
saying.

The critical insight is that this is a _behavioral_ pattern, not a
syntactic one. An agent that passes a narrow rule check (e.g., AP-001
for blanket eslint-disable) may still be engaging in guardrail
suppression if it uses a targeted disable to avoid fixing the
underlying code. The family definition — not any single rule — is what
captures the full concept.

## Why It's Harmful

- Legitimate issues get silenced along with the inconvenient one. A
  blanket `eslint-disable` hides every lint error in scope, not just
  the one that prompted it.
- Future developers inherit suppressed warnings without knowing why.
  The suppression outlives the context that justified it.
- The codebase gradually becomes unprotectable as suppressions
  accumulate. Each one is small; collectively they erode the value of
  the safety tooling.
- AI agents find loopholes at rule boundaries. An agent that knows
  AP-001 catches blanket disables will use targeted disables instead —
  passing the specific check while violating the concept. The flat
  rule format enables this; the family definition prevents it.
- Suppressions compound. Once `@ts-ignore` appears in a codebase,
  it becomes the template for handling every type error — copy the
  pattern, move on. The cost is deferred until someone tries to
  refactor and discovers the type system has been gutted.

## The Spectrum

Rules in this family sit on a severity spectrum from worst to
acceptable:

1. **Blanket suppression** (AP-001, AP-004, GS-002) — Disables ALL
   checks in scope. The worst case. Hides unknown unknowns.
   `/* eslint-disable */` silences every rule; `@ts-ignore` suppresses
   every type error on the next line. The chained type assertion
   `x as unknown as T` (GS-002) belongs here too: widening to `unknown`
   removes the only check a plain `as` still performs — that the two
   types plausibly overlap — so the conversion that follows is
   unconstrained. None of the three tells you what was wrong or why it
   was suppressed.

2. **Targeted suppression without rationale** (AP-002, GS-001) —
   Disables one specific check but doesn't explain why. Better than
   blanket suppression because the scope is narrow, but the next
   person to read the code won't know if this was laziness or
   necessity. The non-null assertion operator (`!`) falls here — it
   overrides nullability for one expression without documenting why
   the value is known to be non-null.

3. **Targeted suppression with rationale** (AP-005) — Disables a
   specific check and documents the reason. `@ts-expect-error` is the
   canonical example: it suppresses one error, fails if the error
   disappears (so it's self-cleaning), and convention encourages a
   comment explaining why. This is acceptable when the code genuinely
   can't satisfy the rule.

## The Right Response

Don't suppress — fix. Read the actual error message and address the
root cause.

The suppression directive is almost never the first thing to try. The
correct sequence is:

1. **Read the error.** What is the tool actually telling you? Many
   suppressions exist because someone didn't read the message.
2. **Fix the code.** Can the underlying issue be resolved? An unused
   variable can be removed. A type mismatch can be corrected. An
   unsafe operation can be guarded.
3. **If the fix isn't possible,** use the narrowest suppression
   available. Disable one rule, not all rules. Use `@ts-expect-error`
   (self-cleaning) over `@ts-ignore` (permanent). Use the non-null
   assertion only when you have a runtime guarantee.
4. **Document why.** Add a comment explaining what the tool is
   complaining about, why the code can't satisfy it, and ideally a
   reference to a tracking issue for the underlying problem.
5. **Ask if this is systemic.** Multiple suppressions of the same
   rule in one file suggest an architectural issue. The rule might be
   wrong for this context, or the code might need restructuring.

Not every hatch in this family is a directive with an error message
attached. A type assertion (`as T`, and especially the chained
`as unknown as T` — GS-002) suppresses a check that never got to
produce a message, so steps 1–2 become: work out why the compiler would
have rejected the plain conversion, and fix *that*. Narrow with a type
guard or a schema parse so the assertion becomes unnecessary; where the
conversion is genuinely deliberate, name it — a small function whose
body carries the one assertion documents the reason the way step 4 asks
a comment to.

## Detection Signals

- `eslint-disable` without a rule name — blanket suppression
- `eslint-disable specific-rule` without an explanatory comment —
  targeted but undocumented
- `@ts-ignore` anywhere outside test files — broad type suppression
- `@ts-expect-error` without an explanatory comment — targeted but
  undocumented
- Non-null assertion (`!`) on a value that could genuinely be null —
  overriding nullability without a runtime check
- `as unknown as T` — a type assertion laundered through `unknown` so
  the compiler's overlap check cannot reject it (GS-002); distinct from
  type-system *evasion* because the author knows the type and is
  overriding it
- Multiple suppressions of the same rule in one file — systemic
  avoidance rather than isolated exception
- `as any` used to override a known type — overlaps with the
  type-system-evasion family and represents the intersection of
  suppression and evasion

## Example

### Observed

```typescript
/* eslint-disable */
const config = loadConfig() as any;
const name = config.user!.name;
```

Three guardrail suppressions in three lines: blanket eslint-disable,
`as any` type assertion, and non-null assertion. Each one hides a
different class of issue. Together they create a zone where no static
analysis tool can help.

### Why it happened

`loadConfig()` returns `unknown` because the config file format isn't
validated. Rather than adding a schema validator, the developer
silenced every tool that complained about the untyped data.

### Better

```typescript
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-assignment -- config schema validated below
const config = loadConfig();
const parsed = ConfigSchema.parse(config);
const name = parsed.user.name;
```

### Best

```typescript
const config = loadConfig();
const parsed = ConfigSchema.parse(config);
const name = parsed.user.name;
// No suppressions needed — the schema validator narrows the type
```
